00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,500
Now we turn to the hardest philosopher

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,000
we're going to read in this course.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:38,500
Today we turn to the hardest philosopher

7
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:39,000
we're going to read in this course.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,160
Let's turn to Immanuel Kant, who offers a different account

9
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,440
of why we have a categorical

10
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:49,960
duty to respect the dignity of persons

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,480
and not to use people as means merely,

12
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:00,000
even for good ends.

13
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:03,000
Kant excelled at the University of

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,840
Konigsberg at the age of 16.

15
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:08,920
At the age of 31, he got his

16
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:13,000
first job as an unsalaried lecturer paid on commission,

17
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,240
based on the number of students

18
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,680
who showed up at his lectures.

19
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:18,960
This is a sensible system that

20
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:20,440
Harvard would do well to consider.

21
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:32,240
Luckily for Kant, he was a

22
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:34,760
popular lecturer and also an industrious one,

23
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:34,800
and he was a very good lecturer.

24
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:34,840
He was a very good lecturer.

25
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:34,920
He was a very good lecturer.

26
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:36,560
And so he eked out a meager living.

27
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:40,320
It wasn't until he was 57

28
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:43,280
that he published his first major work,

29
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:45,640
but it was worth the wait.

30
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,200
The book was The Critique of Pure Reason,

31
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:52,440
perhaps the most important work in all of modern philosophy.

32
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:55,360
And a few years later, Kant wrote

33
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:58,000
The Groundwork for the Metaphysics of Morals,

34
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:00,320
which we read in this course.

35
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:04,360
I want to acknowledge, even before we start,

36
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:07,120
that Kant is a difficult thinker.

37
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:09,460
But it's important to try

38
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,600
to figure out what he's saying,

39
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:20,440
because what this book is about is, well,

40
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:22,780
it's about what the supreme

41
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:24,920
principle of morality is, number one,

42
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:29,280
and it's also, it gives us an account,

43
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:31,520
one of the most powerful accounts we have,

44
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,380
of what freedom really is.

45
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:34,320
So,

46
00:02:34,519 --> 00:02:36,320
let me start today.

47
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:39,959
Kant rejects utilitarianism.

48
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:44,680
He thinks that

49
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:49,519
the individual person,

50
00:02:50,799 --> 00:02:52,040
all human beings,

51
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:55,720
have a certain dignity

52
00:02:57,280 --> 00:02:59,079
that commands our respect.

53
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:03,959
The reason the individual is sacred,

54
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:04,960
and the individual is a

55
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:05,960
bearer of rights according to Kant,

56
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,720
doesn't stem from the idea that we own ourselves,

57
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:13,920
but instead from the idea

58
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:17,920
that we are all rational beings.

59
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:24,120
We're all rational beings, which simply means

60
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:29,120
that we are beings who are capable of reason.

61
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:33,080
We are also autonomous beings,

62
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,320
autonomous beings,

63
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:40,280
which is to say that we are

64
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:44,040
beings capable of acting and choosing freely.

65
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:48,640
Now, this capacity for reason and freedom

66
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:52,000
isn't the only capacity we have.

67
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,240
We also have the capacity for pain and pleasure,

68
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:58,600
for suffering and satisfaction.

69
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:01,560
Kant admits,

70
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:03,040
the utilitarians were

71
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:04,080
half right.

72
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:07,280
Of course,

73
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:10,880
we seek to avoid pain,

74
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:13,280
and we like pleasure.

75
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:17,040
Kant doesn't deny this.

76
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,600
What he does deny

77
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:22,400
is Bentham's claim

78
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:27,200
that pain and pleasure are our sovereign masters.

79
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:28,720
He thinks that's wrong.

80
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:32,880
Kant thinks

81
00:04:33,080 --> 00:04:38,360
that it's our rational capacity that makes us distinctive,

82
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:39,440
that makes us special,

83
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:45,880
that sets us apart from and above mere animal existence.

84
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:50,480
It makes us something more

85
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:53,680
than just physical creatures with appetites.

86
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:55,480
Now,

87
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:58,159
we often think

88
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:01,880
of freedom as simply consisting

89
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:02,680
of freedom as simply consisting

90
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:05,680
of doing what we want,

91
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:07,920
or in the absence of

92
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,960
obstacles to getting what we want.

93
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:13,320
That's one way of thinking about freedom.

94
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:17,760
But this isn't Kant's idea of freedom.

95
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:20,860
Kant has a more stringent, demanding notion

96
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:23,760
of what it means to be free.

97
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:25,860
And though it's stringent and

98
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:27,760
demanding, if you think it through,

99
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:31,680
it's actually pretty persuasive.

100
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:34,160
Kant reasons as follows.

101
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:36,840
When we, like animals,

102
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:38,840
seek after pleasure,

103
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,860
or the satisfaction of our

104
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:42,680
desires, or the avoidance of pain,

105
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:48,760
when we do that, we aren't really acting freely.

106
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:51,000
Why not?

107
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:53,200
We're really acting

108
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:59,680
as the slaves of those appetites and impulses.

109
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:01,560
I didn't choose

110
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:05,800
this particular hunger or that particular appetite.

111
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,160
And so when I act to satisfy it,

112
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:14,720
I'm just acting according to natural necessity.

113
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:21,640
And for Kant, freedom is the opposite of necessity.

114
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:26,920
There was an advertising slogan for the

115
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:30,800
soft drink Sprite a few years ago.

116
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,560
The slogan was,

117
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:34,760
Obey your thirst.

118
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:39,360
There's a Kantian insight buried

119
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:43,760
in that Sprite advertising slogan.

120
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:46,160
That, in a way, is Kant's point.

121
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:49,360
When you go for Sprite or Pepsi,

122
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:52,740
you're really, you might think that

123
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:55,920
you're choosing freely Sprite versus Pepsi,

124
00:06:56,120 --> 00:07:00,720
but you're actually obeying something, a thirst.

125
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:05,480
Or maybe a desire manufactured or massaged by advertising.

126
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:09,780
You're obeying a prompting that

127
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:13,880
you yourself haven't chosen or created.

128
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:18,520
And here it's worth

129
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:24,240
noticing Kant's specially demanding idea of freedom.

130
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:27,320
What way of acting?

131
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:30,720
How can my will be determined, if not by

132
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:33,800
the promptings of nature or my

133
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:36,680
hunger or my appetite or my desires?

134
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:43,560
Kant's answer, to act freely is to act autonomously.

135
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:46,240
And to act autonomously is to act

136
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:48,720
according to a law that I give myself,

137
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:50,840
not according

138
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:53,680
to the physical laws of nature or

139
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:56,320
to the laws of cause and effect,

140
00:07:56,520 --> 00:08:00,720
which include my desire to eat or to drink or

141
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:07,240
choose this food in a restaurant over that.

142
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:09,200
Now, what is the opposite?

143
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:14,360
What is the opposite of autonomy?

144
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:20,940
For Kant, he invents a special

145
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:25,440
term to describe the opposite of autonomy.

146
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:29,720
Heteronomy is the opposite of autonomy.

147
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:30,760
When I act

148
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:37,679
heteronymously, I'm acting according to an inclination

149
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:42,360
or a desire that I haven't chosen for myself.

150
00:08:42,559 --> 00:08:45,440
So freedom as autonomy

151
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:53,960
is the specially stringent idea that Kant insists on.

152
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:58,200
Now, why is autonomy

153
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:00,720
the opposite of acting heteronymously or

154
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:04,040
according to the dictates of nature?

155
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:08,880
Kant's point is that nature is governed by laws,

156
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:12,760
laws of cause and effect, for example.

157
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:17,880
Suppose you drop a billiard ball.

158
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:21,120
It falls to the ground.

159
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:25,560
We wouldn't say the billiard ball is acting freely.

160
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:26,720
Why not?

161
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:28,640
It's acting according to the law of nature,

162
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:30,600
according to the laws

163
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:33,800
of cause and effect, the law of gravity.

164
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,400
And just as he has an

165
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:40,800
unusually demanding and stringent conception of freedom,

166
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:45,540
freedom as autonomy, he also

167
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:50,080
has a demanding conception of morality.

168
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:53,460
To act freely is not to choose

169
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:56,640
the best means to a given end.

170
00:09:56,840 --> 00:10:00,600
It's to choose the end itself for its own

171
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:03,360
sake.

172
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:06,560
And that's something

173
00:10:06,760 --> 00:10:09,280
that human beings can do

174
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:13,120
and that billiard balls can't.

175
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:17,560
Insofar as we act on inclination or pursue pleasure,

176
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:21,120
we act as means to the

177
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:24,480
realization of ends given outside us.

178
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:28,720
We are instruments rather than authors

179
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:29,760
of the purposes

180
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:32,000
we pursue.

181
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:33,840
That's

182
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:37,319
the heteronymous determination of the will.

183
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:40,319
On the other hand, insofar as we act autonomously,

184
00:10:40,520 --> 00:10:43,200
according to a law we give ourselves,

185
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:45,480
we do something for its own

186
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:47,560
sake, as an end in itself.

187
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:49,400
When we act autonomously,

188
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:53,680
we cease to be instruments to purposes given outside us.

189
00:10:53,880 --> 00:10:55,560
We become,

190
00:10:55,760 --> 00:10:58,520
or we can come to think of ourselves,

191
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:02,600
as ends in ourselves.

192
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:06,160
This capacity to act freely,

193
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:08,700
Kant tells us, is what

194
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:11,040
gives human life its special dignity.

195
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:14,560
Respecting human dignity means regarding

196
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:17,880
persons not just as means,

197
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:21,200
but also as ends in themselves.

198
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:24,780
And this is why it's wrong to use

199
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:28,160
people for the sake of other people's well-being

200
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:29,600
or happiness.

201
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:33,500
This is the real reason,

202
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:37,200
Kant says, that utilitarianism goes wrong.

203
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,500
This is the reason it's important

204
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:41,600
to respect the dignity of persons

205
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:46,000
and to uphold their rights.

206
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:47,900
So even if there are cases,

207
00:11:47,900 --> 00:11:49,600
remember, John Stuart Mill said, well,

208
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:52,119
in the long run, if we uphold

209
00:11:52,119 --> 00:11:54,439
justice and respect the dignity of persons,

210
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:57,600
we will maximize human happiness.

211
00:11:57,800 --> 00:11:59,600
What would Kant's answer be to that?

212
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:02,200
What would his answer be?

213
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:04,700
Even if that were true, even

214
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:07,000
if the calculus worked out that way,

215
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:11,720
even if you shouldn't throw the Christians to the lions,

216
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:14,040
because in the long run, fear

217
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:16,160
will spread, the overall utility will decline,

218
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:18,780
the utilitarian would be upholding justice

219
00:12:18,780 --> 00:12:21,200
and rights and respect for persons

220
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:27,600
for the wrong reason, for a purely contingent reason,

221
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:29,680
for an instrumental

222
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:31,240
reason.

223
00:12:31,439 --> 00:12:33,619
It would still be using people,

224
00:12:33,619 --> 00:12:35,800
even where the calculus works out

225
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,060
for the best in the long run,

226
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:42,120
it would still be using people as means

227
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:49,360
rather than respecting them as ends in themselves.

228
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:53,840
So that's Kant's idea of freedom is autonomy.

229
00:12:54,040 --> 00:12:56,720
And you can begin to see how

230
00:12:56,720 --> 00:12:59,400
it's connected to his idea of morality.

231
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:04,040
But we still have to answer one more question.

232
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:06,580
What gives an act its

233
00:13:06,580 --> 00:13:08,920
moral worth in the first place?

234
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:11,800
If it can't be directed

235
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,740
at utility or satisfying wants and desires,

236
00:13:15,740 --> 00:13:19,480
what gives an action its moral worth?

237
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:22,280
This leads us from Kant's demanding idea

238
00:13:22,480 --> 00:13:27,880
of freedom to his demanding idea of morality.

239
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:30,200
What does Kant say?

240
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:36,000
What makes an action morally worthy

241
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:38,580
consists not in the consequences or

242
00:13:38,580 --> 00:13:40,960
in the results that flow from it.

243
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:45,280
What makes an action morally worthy has to do

244
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:47,920
with the motive,

245
00:13:48,120 --> 00:13:53,440
with the quality of the will, with the intention

246
00:13:53,640 --> 00:13:56,000
for which the act is done.

247
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
What matters is the motive.

248
00:13:59,200 --> 00:14:04,000
And the motive must be of a certain kind.

249
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:06,860
So the moral worth of an action

250
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:09,520
depends on the motive for which it's done.

251
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:13,720
And the important thing is that the person

252
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:17,720
do the right thing for the right reason.

253
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:21,020
A good will isn't good because of

254
00:14:21,020 --> 00:14:24,120
what it affects or accomplishes, Kant writes.

255
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:25,920
It's good in itself.

256
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:28,700
Even if by its utmost

257
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:31,200
effort, the good will accomplishes nothing,

258
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:33,000
it would still shine like

259
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:34,600
a jewel for its own sake

260
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:38,000
as something which has its full value in itself.

261
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:42,200
And so for any action to be morally good,

262
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:45,200
it's not enough that it

263
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:48,000
should conform to the moral law.

264
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:49,900
It must also be done for

265
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:51,600
the sake of the moral law.

266
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:55,800
The idea is that the motive

267
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:00,600
that confers the moral worth on an action.

268
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:03,700
And the only kind of motive that

269
00:15:03,700 --> 00:15:06,600
can confirm moral worth on an action

270
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:09,600
is the motive of duty.

271
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:12,800
Well, what's the opposite of doing something out

272
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:15,800
of a sense of duty because it's right?

273
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Well, for Kant, the opposite would be all

274
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
of those motives having to do with our inclinations.

275
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:25,200
And inclinations refer to all of our

276
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:28,700
desires, all of our contingently given

277
00:15:28,700 --> 00:15:32,200
wants, preferences, impulses, and the like.

278
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:34,900
Only actions done for the sake of

279
00:15:34,900 --> 00:15:37,400
the moral law, for the sake of duty,

280
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:40,600
only these actions have moral worth.

281
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:42,800
Now I want to see

282
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:44,800
what you think about this idea,

283
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
but first let's consider a few examples.

284
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:52,200
Kant begins with an example of a shopkeeper.

285
00:15:52,400 --> 00:15:55,120
He wants to bring out the idea that,

286
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:59,320
by the intuition, and make plausible the idea

287
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:02,020
that what confers moral worth on an action

288
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:04,520
is that it be done because it's right.

289
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:07,720
He says, suppose there's a shopkeeper,

290
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:10,920
and an inexperienced customer comes in.

291
00:16:11,120 --> 00:16:13,120
The shopkeeper knows that he could

292
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:15,120
give the customer the wrong change,

293
00:16:15,320 --> 00:16:18,320
could shortchange the customer and get away with it.

294
00:16:18,520 --> 00:16:22,520
At least that customer wouldn't know.

295
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:23,920
But the shopkeeper knows that he's not the one

296
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:25,120
who's going to give the customer the wrong change.

297
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:29,320
He nonetheless says, well, if I shortchange this customer,

298
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:32,520
word may get out, my reputation would

299
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:35,520
be damaged, and I would lose business.

300
00:16:35,720 --> 00:16:39,720
So I won't shortchange this customer.

301
00:16:39,920 --> 00:16:42,920
The shopkeeper does nothing wrong.

302
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:45,120
He gives the correct change.

303
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:48,320
But does his action have moral worth?

304
00:16:48,520 --> 00:16:50,200
Kant says no.

305
00:16:50,400 --> 00:16:52,360
It doesn't have moral worth, because

306
00:16:52,360 --> 00:16:54,320
the shopkeeper only did the right thing

307
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:58,440
for the wrong reason, out of self-interest.

308
00:16:58,640 --> 00:17:03,640
That's a pretty straightforward case.

309
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:10,839
Then he takes another case, the case of suicide.

310
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:16,040
He says we have a duty to preserve ourselves.

311
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:20,240
Now, for most people who

312
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:24,240
love life, we have multiple reasons.

313
00:17:24,439 --> 00:17:28,439
For not taking our own lives.

314
00:17:28,639 --> 00:17:32,639
So the only way we can really tell,

315
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:36,919
the only way we can isolate the operative motive

316
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:39,919
for someone who doesn't take his or her life,

317
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:45,120
is to think, to imagine someone who's miserable.

318
00:17:46,919 --> 00:17:51,720
And who, despite

319
00:17:51,919 --> 00:17:54,240
having an absolutely miserable life,

320
00:17:54,439 --> 00:17:58,439
nonetheless,

321
00:17:58,639 --> 00:18:01,639
recognizes the duty to preserve oneself,

322
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:05,840
and so does not commit suicide.

323
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:11,040
That's the force of the example,

324
00:18:11,240 --> 00:18:16,240
is to bring out the motive that matters.

325
00:18:16,439 --> 00:18:19,339
And the motive that matters for morality is

326
00:18:19,339 --> 00:18:22,240
doing the right thing for the sake of duty.

327
00:18:22,439 --> 00:18:24,280
Let me just give you

328
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:27,439
a couple of other examples.

329
00:18:27,639 --> 00:18:29,040
The Better Business Bureau.

330
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:30,639
What's their slogan?

331
00:18:30,840 --> 00:18:32,639
The slogan of the Better Business Bureau?

332
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:35,840
Honesty is the best policy.

333
00:18:36,040 --> 00:18:38,439
It's also the most profitable.

334
00:18:38,639 --> 00:18:41,639
This is the Better Business Bureau's

335
00:18:41,639 --> 00:18:44,639
full-page ad in the New York Times.

336
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:46,840
Honesty.

337
00:18:47,040 --> 00:18:50,040
It's as important as any other asset.

338
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:52,240
Because a business that deals

339
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:54,240
in truth, openness, and fair value,

340
00:18:54,439 --> 00:18:57,639
cannot help but do well.

341
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:00,840
Come join us and profit from it.

342
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:05,040
What would Kant say about the moral worth

343
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:07,240
of the honest dealings of

344
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:09,240
members of the Better Business Bureau?

345
00:19:09,439 --> 00:19:10,439
What would he say?

346
00:19:10,639 --> 00:19:12,639
That here's a perfect example,

347
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:16,739
that if this is the reason that

348
00:19:16,739 --> 00:19:20,639
these companies deal honestly with their customers,

349
00:19:20,840 --> 00:19:24,240
their action lacks moral worth.

350
00:19:24,439 --> 00:19:26,560
This is Kant's point.

351
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:28,879
A couple of years ago at the University of Maryland,

352
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:31,360
there was a problem with cheating.

353
00:19:31,560 --> 00:19:37,280
And so they initiated an honor system.

354
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:40,360
And they created a program with local merchants

355
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:42,760
that if you signed the honor

356
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:44,960
pledge, a pledge not to cheat,

357
00:19:45,159 --> 00:19:48,559
you would get discounts of

358
00:19:48,559 --> 00:19:51,960
10 to 25% at local shops.

359
00:19:52,159 --> 00:19:53,439
Well, what would you think of someone

360
00:19:53,640 --> 00:19:56,560
motivated to uphold an honor

361
00:19:56,560 --> 00:19:59,480
code with the hope of discounts?

362
00:19:59,680 --> 00:20:04,200
It's the same as Kant's shopkeeper.

363
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:06,140
The point is, what matters

364
00:20:06,140 --> 00:20:07,880
is the quality of the will,

365
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:10,820
the character of the motive,

366
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:13,560
and the relevant motive to morality

367
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:17,660
can only be the motive of

368
00:20:17,660 --> 00:20:21,560
duty, not the motive of inclination.

369
00:20:21,760 --> 00:20:23,440
And when I act out of duty,

370
00:20:23,640 --> 00:20:28,200
when I resist as my motive for acting,

371
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:32,640
inclinations or self-interest, even sympathy and altruism,

372
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:37,020
only then am I acting freely,

373
00:20:37,020 --> 00:20:41,200
only then am I acting autonomously.

374
00:20:41,400 --> 00:20:45,580
Only then is my will not

375
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:49,760
determined or governed by external considerations.

376
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:53,360
That's the link between Kant's idea of freedom

377
00:20:53,640 --> 00:20:54,440
and of morality.

378
00:20:54,640 --> 00:20:56,600
Now, I want to pause here to

379
00:20:56,600 --> 00:20:58,560
see if all of this is clear,

380
00:20:58,760 --> 00:21:03,120
or if you have some questions or puzzles.

381
00:21:03,320 --> 00:21:05,900
They can be questions of

382
00:21:05,900 --> 00:21:08,480
clarification, or they can be challenges.

383
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:11,320
If you want to challenge this idea

384
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:13,700
that only the motive of duty

385
00:21:13,700 --> 00:21:15,880
confers moral worth on the action.

386
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:19,280
What do you think? Yes.

387
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:23,440
Yeah, I actually have two questions of clarification.

388
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:26,480
The first is, there seems to be an

389
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:29,320
aspect of this that makes it sort of

390
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:32,080
self-defeating in that once you're

391
00:21:32,080 --> 00:21:34,640
conscious of what morality is,

392
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:37,260
you can sort of alter your

393
00:21:37,260 --> 00:21:39,680
motive to achieve that end of morality.

394
00:21:39,880 --> 00:21:41,700
And secondly... Give me an example

395
00:21:41,700 --> 00:21:43,520
of what you have in mind.

396
00:21:43,720 --> 00:21:46,180
The shopkeeper example, if he decides

397
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:48,640
that he wants to give the person

398
00:21:48,840 --> 00:21:51,140
the money to do the right thing, and

399
00:21:51,140 --> 00:21:53,440
he decides that's his motive to do so,

400
00:21:53,640 --> 00:21:55,140
because he wants to be moral,

401
00:21:55,140 --> 00:21:56,640
then isn't that sort of defeating,

402
00:21:56,840 --> 00:21:58,960
trying to... Isn't that sort of

403
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:01,080
defeating the purity of his action?

404
00:22:01,280 --> 00:22:03,960
If morality is determined by his motive,

405
00:22:03,960 --> 00:22:06,640
his motive is then to act morally.

406
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:09,560
So you're imagining a case not

407
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:12,280
of the purely selfish calculating shopkeeper,

408
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:15,360
but of one who says, well,

409
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:18,240
he may consider shortchanging the customer,

410
00:22:18,440 --> 00:22:20,900
but then he says, not, well,

411
00:22:20,900 --> 00:22:23,360
my reputation might suffer if word gets

412
00:22:23,560 --> 00:22:26,520
out, but instead he says, actually, I

413
00:22:26,520 --> 00:22:29,480
would like to be the kind of honest

414
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:32,460
person who gives the right change

415
00:22:32,460 --> 00:22:35,240
to customers simply because it's the right

416
00:22:35,440 --> 00:22:37,200
thing to do. Or simply

417
00:22:37,200 --> 00:22:38,960
because I want to be moral.

418
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:40,960
Because I want to be moral.

419
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:42,760
I want to be a good person.

420
00:22:42,960 --> 00:22:45,200
And so I'm going to conform all

421
00:22:45,200 --> 00:22:47,440
of my actions to what morality requires.

422
00:22:53,640 --> 00:22:57,200
A little point. It's a good question.

423
00:22:57,400 --> 00:23:00,120
Kant does acknowledge, you're pressing

424
00:23:00,120 --> 00:23:02,840
Kant on an important point here,

425
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:07,400
Kant does say there has to be

426
00:23:07,400 --> 00:23:11,760
some incentive to obey the moral law.

427
00:23:11,960 --> 00:23:15,760
It can't be a self-interested incentive

428
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:19,560
that would defeat it by definition.

429
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:23,360
So he speaks of a different kind of incentive

430
00:23:23,560 --> 00:23:27,320
and inclination. He speaks of reverence for the moral law.

431
00:23:27,520 --> 00:23:31,240
So if that shopkeeper says,

432
00:23:31,440 --> 00:23:33,720
I want to develop

433
00:23:33,920 --> 00:23:37,440
a reverence for the moral law,

434
00:23:37,640 --> 00:23:41,800
and so I'm going to do the right thing,

435
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:43,880
then I think he's there.

436
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:47,720
He's there as far as Kant's concerned,

437
00:23:47,720 --> 00:23:51,360
because he's formed his motive, his will,

438
00:23:51,560 --> 00:23:53,360
is conforming

439
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:56,380
to the moral law once

440
00:23:56,380 --> 00:23:59,120
he sees the importance of it.

441
00:23:59,320 --> 00:24:01,840
So it would count. It would count.

442
00:24:02,040 --> 00:24:04,800
All right. And then secondly, very quickly,

443
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,020
what stops morality from becoming

444
00:24:07,020 --> 00:24:09,040
completely objective in this point?

445
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:12,400
What stops morality from becoming completely subjective?

446
00:24:12,600 --> 00:24:15,360
Like how can if there's if

447
00:24:15,360 --> 00:24:18,120
morally is if morality is completely determined

448
00:24:18,320 --> 00:24:20,040
by your morals, then how can you

449
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:21,760
apply this or how can it be enforced?

450
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:22,960
That's also a great question.

451
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:23,360
What's your name?

452
00:24:23,640 --> 00:24:24,680
My name is Amadi.

453
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:26,640
Amadi.

454
00:24:26,840 --> 00:24:28,320
All right.

455
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:33,680
If acting morally means acting according

456
00:24:34,120 --> 00:24:37,080
to a moral law out of duty,

457
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:40,500
and if it's also to act

458
00:24:40,500 --> 00:24:43,720
freely in the sense of autonomously,

459
00:24:43,920 --> 00:24:46,080
it must mean that I'm acting according

460
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:48,240
to a law that I give myself.

461
00:24:48,440 --> 00:24:51,040
That's what it means to act autonomously.

462
00:24:51,240 --> 00:24:52,560
Amadi is right about that.

463
00:24:53,640 --> 00:24:58,920
But that does raise a really interesting question.

464
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:01,480
If acting autonomously means acting according

465
00:25:01,480 --> 00:25:03,840
to a law I give myself,

466
00:25:04,040 --> 00:25:07,320
that's how I escape the chain of

467
00:25:07,320 --> 00:25:10,600
cause and effect and the laws of nature.

468
00:25:10,800 --> 00:25:13,240
What's to guarantee

469
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:16,800
that the law I give myself

470
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:21,440
when I'm acting out of duty is the same

471
00:25:21,640 --> 00:25:23,400
as the law that Amadi is giving him?

472
00:25:23,640 --> 00:25:25,640
What's to guarantee that the law I give myself

473
00:25:25,840 --> 00:25:26,940
is the same as the

474
00:25:26,940 --> 00:25:28,040
law that Amadi is giving himself

475
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:33,640
and that each of you gives yourselves?

476
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:35,360
Well, here's the question.

477
00:25:35,560 --> 00:25:38,600
How many moral laws, from Kant's point

478
00:25:38,600 --> 00:25:41,640
of view, are there in this room?

479
00:25:41,840 --> 00:25:45,640
Are there a thousand or is there one?

480
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:47,820
He thinks there's one, which in a

481
00:25:47,820 --> 00:25:49,800
way does go back to this question.

482
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:51,360
All right. What is the moral law?

483
00:25:51,560 --> 00:25:53,440
What does it tell us?

484
00:25:53,640 --> 00:25:57,360
So what guarantees?

485
00:25:57,560 --> 00:25:59,800
It sounds like it to act autonomously

486
00:25:59,800 --> 00:26:02,040
is to act according to one's conscience,

487
00:26:02,240 --> 00:26:04,600
according to a law one gives oneself.

488
00:26:04,800 --> 00:26:08,640
But what guarantees that if we all exercise our reason,

489
00:26:08,840 --> 00:26:10,060
we will come up with

490
00:26:10,060 --> 00:26:11,280
one and the same moral law?

491
00:26:11,480 --> 00:26:14,640
That's what Amadi wants to know.

492
00:26:15,480 --> 00:26:18,480
Here's Kant's answer.

493
00:26:20,040 --> 00:26:23,440
The reason that leads us,

494
00:26:23,640 --> 00:26:27,760
to the law we give ourselves as autonomous beings,

495
00:26:27,960 --> 00:26:29,960
is a reason,

496
00:26:33,280 --> 00:26:35,780
it's a kind of practical reason

497
00:26:35,780 --> 00:26:38,280
that we share as human beings.

498
00:26:40,640 --> 00:26:44,640
It's not idiosyncratic.

499
00:26:46,400 --> 00:26:49,400
The reason we need to respect the dignity of persons

500
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:50,840
is that we're all rational beings.

501
00:26:51,040 --> 00:26:52,560
We all have the capacity for reason.

502
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:56,120
And it's the exercise of that capacity for reason,

503
00:26:56,320 --> 00:27:02,040
which exists undifferentiated in all of us,

504
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:05,800
that makes us worthy of dignity, all of us.

505
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:11,000
And since it's the same capacity for reason,

506
00:27:13,919 --> 00:27:16,259
unqualified by particular

507
00:27:16,259 --> 00:27:18,600
autobiographies and life circumstances,

508
00:27:18,800 --> 00:27:21,360
it's the same universal capacity for reason

509
00:27:21,560 --> 00:27:23,600
that delivers the moral law.

510
00:27:23,800 --> 00:27:26,639
It turns out that to act autonomously

511
00:27:26,840 --> 00:27:29,760
is to act according to a law we give ourselves,

512
00:27:29,959 --> 00:27:32,560
exercising our reason,

513
00:27:32,760 --> 00:27:35,440
but it's the reason we

514
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:38,120
share with everyone as rational beings,

515
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:41,360
not the particular reasons we have given our upbringings,

516
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:45,360
our particular values, our particular interests.

517
00:27:45,560 --> 00:27:50,240
It's pure practical reason in Kant's terms,

518
00:27:50,439 --> 00:27:51,280
which legislates

519
00:27:51,399 --> 00:27:55,099
a priori, regardless of any

520
00:27:55,099 --> 00:27:58,800
particular contingent or empirical ends.

521
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:04,600
Well, what moral law would that kind of reason deliver?

522
00:28:06,800 --> 00:28:09,440
What is its content?

523
00:28:09,639 --> 00:28:11,600
To answer that question,

524
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:13,399
you have to read the groundwork,

525
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:16,399
and we'll continue with that question next time.

526
00:28:21,480 --> 00:28:23,240
We'll continue with that question next time.

527
00:28:23,440 --> 00:28:24,360
Thank you.

528
00:28:24,520 --> 00:28:26,000
Thank you, all.

529
00:28:26,200 --> 00:28:27,520
Thank you.

530
00:28:27,680 --> 00:28:30,020
For Kant, morally speaking, suicide

531
00:28:30,020 --> 00:28:32,360
is on a par with murder.

532
00:28:32,560 --> 00:28:36,000
It's on a par with murder because what we violate

533
00:28:37,399 --> 00:28:38,960
when we take a life,

534
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:43,200
when we take someone's life, ours or somebody else's,

535
00:28:43,639 --> 00:28:45,879
we use that person,

536
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:48,640
we use a rational being,

537
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:51,200
we use humanity as a means,

538
00:28:51,280 --> 00:28:55,280
And so we fail to respect humanity as an end.

539
00:29:02,780 --> 00:29:05,780
Today we turn back to Kant before we do...

540
00:29:08,780 --> 00:29:13,780
Remember, this is the week by the end of which

541
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:16,530
all of you will basically get

542
00:29:16,530 --> 00:29:18,780
Kant, figure out what he's up to.

543
00:29:18,780 --> 00:29:22,780
You're laughing. No, it will happen.

544
00:29:27,280 --> 00:29:33,280
Kant's groundwork is about two big questions.

545
00:29:35,280 --> 00:29:40,280
First, what is the supreme principle of morality?

546
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:45,280
Second, how is freedom possible?

547
00:29:48,780 --> 00:29:50,780
Two big questions.

548
00:29:51,280 --> 00:29:56,030
Now, one way of making your

549
00:29:56,030 --> 00:30:00,780
way through this dense philosophical book

550
00:30:01,280 --> 00:30:06,030
is to bear in mind a set of

551
00:30:06,030 --> 00:30:10,780
oppositions or contrasts or dualisms that are related.

552
00:30:14,280 --> 00:30:17,280
Today I'd like to talk about them.

553
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:18,280
Today I'd like to talk about them.

554
00:30:18,780 --> 00:30:19,780
Today we're going to answer the question,

555
00:30:20,780 --> 00:30:22,780
what, according to Kant, is

556
00:30:22,780 --> 00:30:24,780
the supreme principle of morality?

557
00:30:26,780 --> 00:30:29,280
And in answering that question, in working our

558
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:31,780
way up to Kant's answer to that question,

559
00:30:34,780 --> 00:30:39,280
it will help to bear in mind

560
00:30:39,280 --> 00:30:43,780
three contrasts or dualisms that Kant sets out.

561
00:30:44,780 --> 00:30:46,780
The first, you'll remember,

562
00:30:47,280 --> 00:30:51,780
had to do with the

563
00:30:51,780 --> 00:30:56,280
motive according to which we act.

564
00:30:57,280 --> 00:31:01,280
And according to Kant, only one

565
00:31:01,280 --> 00:31:05,280
kind of motive is consistent with morality.

566
00:31:06,280 --> 00:31:08,280
The motive of duty.

567
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:11,280
Doing the right thing for the right reason.

568
00:31:13,280 --> 00:31:15,280
What other kind of motives are there?

569
00:31:15,280 --> 00:31:21,780
Kant sums them up in the category of inclination.

570
00:31:22,780 --> 00:31:28,280
Every time the motive for what we do is to

571
00:31:28,280 --> 00:31:33,780
satisfy a desire or a preference that we may have

572
00:31:34,780 --> 00:31:37,780
to pursue some interest, we're acting out of inclination.

573
00:31:38,780 --> 00:31:41,780
Now, let me pause to see if,

574
00:31:41,780 --> 00:31:44,280
in thinking about the question of

575
00:31:44,280 --> 00:31:46,780
the motive of duty, the good will,

576
00:31:47,280 --> 00:31:49,530
see if any of you has a

577
00:31:49,530 --> 00:31:51,780
question about that much of Kant's claim.

578
00:31:53,280 --> 00:31:55,780
Or is everybody happy with this distinction?

579
00:31:56,280 --> 00:31:57,780
What do you think?

580
00:31:58,780 --> 00:31:59,780
Go ahead.

581
00:32:00,280 --> 00:32:02,780
When you make that distinction between duty and inclination,

582
00:32:03,280 --> 00:32:05,280
is there ever any moral action, ever?

583
00:32:05,780 --> 00:32:07,530
I mean, you could always, kind of,

584
00:32:07,530 --> 00:32:09,280
probably find some selfish motive, can't you?

585
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:11,780
Maybe very often people do

586
00:32:11,780 --> 00:32:14,280
have self-interested motives when they act.

587
00:32:14,780 --> 00:32:17,280
Kant wouldn't dispute that.

588
00:32:18,780 --> 00:32:23,030
But what Kant is saying is

589
00:32:23,030 --> 00:32:27,280
that insofar as we act morally,

590
00:32:27,780 --> 00:32:31,280
that is, insofar as our actions have moral worth,

591
00:32:31,780 --> 00:32:35,280
what confers moral worth is precisely

592
00:32:35,280 --> 00:32:38,280
our capacity to rise above

593
00:32:38,280 --> 00:32:41,280
self-interest and prudence and inclination

594
00:32:41,780 --> 00:32:44,280
and to act out of duty.

595
00:32:46,780 --> 00:32:51,780
Some years ago I read about a spelling bee.

596
00:32:52,780 --> 00:32:55,780
And there was a young man who

597
00:32:55,780 --> 00:32:58,780
was declared the winner of the spelling bee.

598
00:32:59,780 --> 00:33:02,780
A kid named Andrew, 13 years old.

599
00:33:03,780 --> 00:33:04,780
The winning word,

600
00:33:05,280 --> 00:33:07,280
the word that he was able to spell,

601
00:33:07,780 --> 00:33:09,280
was echolalia.

602
00:33:10,280 --> 00:33:12,280
Does anyone know what echolalia is?

603
00:33:13,280 --> 00:33:14,280
What?

604
00:33:15,280 --> 00:33:17,280
It's not some type of flower, no.

605
00:33:21,280 --> 00:33:23,280
It means the tendency to repeat, as in echo,

606
00:33:23,780 --> 00:33:26,280
to repeat what you've heard.

607
00:33:27,280 --> 00:33:30,280
Anyhow, he misspelled it, actually.

608
00:33:31,280 --> 00:33:32,280
But the judges misheard him.

609
00:33:32,780 --> 00:33:34,280
They thought he had spelled it correctly

610
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:36,780
and awarded him the championship

611
00:33:36,780 --> 00:33:38,780
of the national spelling bee.

612
00:33:39,780 --> 00:33:45,780
And he went to the judges afterward and said,

613
00:33:46,780 --> 00:33:48,780
actually, I misspelled it.

614
00:33:48,780 --> 00:33:50,780
I don't deserve the prize.

615
00:33:51,280 --> 00:33:52,780
And he was regarded as a moral hero,

616
00:33:53,280 --> 00:33:55,780
and he was written up in the New York Times,

617
00:33:56,280 --> 00:34:01,780
Miss Speller is a spelling bee hero.

618
00:34:03,280 --> 00:34:04,280
There's Andrew.

619
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:08,279
With his proud mother.

620
00:34:13,279 --> 00:34:16,279
And when he was interviewed afterwards,

621
00:34:16,279 --> 00:34:17,279
listen to this,

622
00:34:17,279 --> 00:34:19,279
when he was interviewed afterwards,

623
00:34:19,779 --> 00:34:21,279
he said, quote,

624
00:34:21,779 --> 00:34:24,279
the judges said I had a lot of integrity.

625
00:34:24,779 --> 00:34:26,529
But then he added that

626
00:34:26,529 --> 00:34:28,279
part of his motive was, quote,

627
00:34:28,779 --> 00:34:31,779
I didn't want to feel like a slime.

628
00:34:32,780 --> 00:34:33,780
I had to think about it.

629
00:34:33,780 --> 00:34:34,780
I had to think about it.

630
00:34:34,780 --> 00:34:36,280
He was a very, very smart person.

631
00:34:36,280 --> 00:34:37,280
And he got to say it.

632
00:34:37,280 --> 00:34:38,280
And I'm a big fan of his words.

633
00:34:38,280 --> 00:34:39,280
I just don't want to hear his thing.

634
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:40,280
All right.

635
00:34:40,280 --> 00:34:41,280
What would Kant say?

636
00:34:41,280 --> 00:34:42,280
Go ahead.

637
00:34:42,280 --> 00:34:43,280
I guess it would depend on whether or not

638
00:34:43,280 --> 00:34:44,280
that was a marginal reason or the predominant reason

639
00:34:44,280 --> 00:34:45,280
in whether or not in why he decided to confess

640
00:34:45,280 --> 00:34:46,280
that he didn't actually spell the word correctly.

641
00:34:46,280 --> 00:34:47,280
Good.

642
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:48,280
And what's your name?

643
00:34:48,280 --> 00:34:48,780
Vosko.

644
00:34:48,780 --> 00:34:49,280
That's very interesting.

645
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:49,780
Is there anyone else who has a view about this?

646
00:34:49,780 --> 00:34:50,780
Does this show that Kant is a liberal?

647
00:34:50,780 --> 00:34:51,280
I think, I think it does.

648
00:34:51,280 --> 00:34:52,280
I think he's a good answer.

649
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:53,280
I think, I think he's a good answer.

650
00:34:53,280 --> 00:34:57,280
That's very interesting. Is there anyone

651
00:34:57,280 --> 00:35:01,280
else who has a view about this?

652
00:35:01,280 --> 00:35:04,280
Does this show that Kant's

653
00:35:04,280 --> 00:35:07,280
principle is too stringent, too demanding?

654
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:13,280
What would Kant say about this? Yes.

655
00:35:13,280 --> 00:35:17,780
I think that Kant actually says that it is the pure

656
00:35:17,780 --> 00:35:22,280
motivation that comes out of duty which gives the action moral growth.

657
00:35:22,280 --> 00:35:24,280
So it's like, for example, in this

658
00:35:24,280 --> 00:35:26,280
case, he might have more than one motive.

659
00:35:26,280 --> 00:35:30,280
He might have the motive of not feeling like a slime, and he might

660
00:35:30,280 --> 00:35:34,280
have the motive of doing the right thing in and of itself, out of duty.

661
00:35:34,280 --> 00:35:38,780
And so while there's more than one motivation going on there, it does not mean

662
00:35:38,780 --> 00:35:43,280
that the action is devoid of moral worth just because he has one other motive.

663
00:35:43,280 --> 00:35:45,280
So because the motive which involves duty

664
00:35:45,280 --> 00:35:47,280
is what gives it the moral growth.

665
00:35:47,280 --> 00:35:50,280
Good. And what's your name? Judith.

666
00:35:50,280 --> 00:35:52,280
Well, Judith, I think that your...

667
00:35:52,280 --> 00:35:55,280
Your account actually is true to Kant.

668
00:35:55,280 --> 00:36:01,780
It's fine to have sentiments and feelings that support doing

669
00:36:01,780 --> 00:36:08,280
the right thing, provided they don't provide the reason for acting.

670
00:36:08,280 --> 00:36:12,280
So I think Judith actually has mounted a pretty good defense

671
00:36:12,280 --> 00:36:16,280
of Kant on this question of the motive of duty. Thank you.

672
00:36:16,280 --> 00:36:20,280
Now, let's go back to the three contrasts.

673
00:36:20,280 --> 00:36:21,280
It's the motive of duty.

674
00:36:21,280 --> 00:36:22,280
It's the motive of duty.

675
00:36:22,280 --> 00:36:27,780
It's clear at least what Kant means when he says that for an action to

676
00:36:27,780 --> 00:36:33,280
have moral worth, it must be done for the sake of duty, not out of inclination.

677
00:36:33,280 --> 00:36:39,780
But as we began to see last time, there's a connection between

678
00:36:39,780 --> 00:36:46,280
Kant's stringent notion of morality and his specially demanding understanding of freedom.

679
00:36:46,280 --> 00:36:50,280
And that leads us to the second contrast.

680
00:36:50,280 --> 00:36:54,280
The link between morality and freedom.

681
00:36:54,280 --> 00:36:57,780
The second contrast describes two different

682
00:36:57,780 --> 00:37:01,280
ways that my will can be determined.

683
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:05,280
Autonomously and heteronymously.

684
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:10,280
And according to Kant, I'm only free when

685
00:37:10,280 --> 00:37:15,280
my will is determined autonomously, which means what?

686
00:37:15,280 --> 00:37:18,280
According to a law that I give myself.

687
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:23,780
We must be capable, if we're capable of freedom as autonomy, we must be capable of acting according

688
00:37:23,780 --> 00:37:29,280
not to a law that's given or imposed on us, but according to a law we give ourselves.

689
00:37:29,280 --> 00:37:34,280
But where could such a law come from?

690
00:37:34,280 --> 00:37:37,280
A law that we give ourselves?

691
00:37:37,280 --> 00:37:40,280
Reason.

692
00:37:40,280 --> 00:37:47,280
If reason determines my will,

693
00:37:48,280 --> 00:37:53,780
then the will becomes the power to choose independent

694
00:37:53,780 --> 00:37:59,280
of the dictates of nature or inclination or circumstance.

695
00:37:59,280 --> 00:38:05,280
So, connected with Kant's demanding notions of morality

696
00:38:05,280 --> 00:38:11,280
and freedom is a specially demanding notion of reason.

697
00:38:11,280 --> 00:38:16,280
Well, how can reason determine the will?

698
00:38:16,280 --> 00:38:17,280
There are two ways.

699
00:38:17,280 --> 00:38:20,280
And this leads to the third contrast.

700
00:38:20,280 --> 00:38:25,280
Kant says there are two different commands of reason.

701
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:30,280
And a command of reason, Kant calls an imperative.

702
00:38:30,280 --> 00:38:33,280
An imperative is simply an ought.

703
00:38:33,280 --> 00:38:37,280
One kind of imperative, perhaps the

704
00:38:37,280 --> 00:38:41,280
most familiar kind, is a hypothetical imperative.

705
00:38:41,280 --> 00:38:46,280
Hypothetical imperatives use instrumental reason.

706
00:38:46,280 --> 00:38:54,280
If you want X, then do Y.

707
00:38:54,280 --> 00:38:57,280
It means ends reasoning.

708
00:38:57,280 --> 00:39:01,280
If you want a good business

709
00:39:01,280 --> 00:39:05,280
reputation, then don't shortchange your customers.

710
00:39:05,280 --> 00:39:07,280
Word may get out.

711
00:39:07,280 --> 00:39:10,280
That's a hypothetical imperative.

712
00:39:10,280 --> 00:39:12,280
If the action would be good solely as

713
00:39:12,280 --> 00:39:14,280
a means to something else, Kant writes the imperative,

714
00:39:14,280 --> 00:39:17,280
is hypothetical.

715
00:39:17,280 --> 00:39:21,280
If the action is represented as good in itself,

716
00:39:21,280 --> 00:39:24,280
and therefore as necessary for a

717
00:39:24,280 --> 00:39:27,280
will which of itself accords with reason,

718
00:39:27,280 --> 00:39:33,280
then the imperative is categorical.

719
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:36,280
That's the difference between a

720
00:39:36,280 --> 00:39:39,280
categorical imperative and a hypothetical one.

721
00:39:39,280 --> 00:39:43,280
A categorical imperative commands categorically.

722
00:39:43,280 --> 00:39:47,280
Which just means without reference to

723
00:39:47,280 --> 00:39:51,280
or dependence on any further purpose.

724
00:39:51,280 --> 00:39:55,280
And so you see the

725
00:39:55,280 --> 00:39:59,280
connection among these three parallel contrasts.

726
00:39:59,280 --> 00:40:04,280
To be free in the sense of autonomous requires

727
00:40:04,280 --> 00:40:09,280
that I act not out of a hypothetical imperative,

728
00:40:09,280 --> 00:40:12,280
but out of a categorical imperative.

729
00:40:13,280 --> 00:40:15,780
And so you see by these

730
00:40:15,780 --> 00:40:18,280
three contrasts, Kant reasons his way,

731
00:40:18,280 --> 00:40:21,780
brings us up to his

732
00:40:21,780 --> 00:40:25,280
derivation of the categorical imperative.

733
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:29,280
Well, this leaves us one big question.

734
00:40:29,280 --> 00:40:31,280
What is the categorical imperative?

735
00:40:31,280 --> 00:40:34,280
What is the supreme principle of morality?

736
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:37,280
What does it command of us?

737
00:40:37,280 --> 00:40:42,280
Kant gives three versions, three formulations,

738
00:40:42,280 --> 00:40:45,280
of the categorical imperative.

739
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:47,780
I want to mention two, and

740
00:40:47,780 --> 00:40:50,280
then see what you think of them.

741
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:54,280
The first version, the first formula,

742
00:40:54,280 --> 00:40:59,280
he calls the formula of the universal law.

743
00:40:59,280 --> 00:41:02,280
Act only on that maxim,

744
00:41:02,280 --> 00:41:04,780
whereby you can at the same time

745
00:41:04,780 --> 00:41:07,280
will that it should become a universal law.

746
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:10,280
And by maxim, what does Kant mean?

747
00:41:10,280 --> 00:41:11,280
He means,

748
00:41:11,280 --> 00:41:16,280
a rule that explains the reason for what you're doing.

749
00:41:16,280 --> 00:41:18,280
A principle.

750
00:41:18,280 --> 00:41:21,280
For example, promise keeping.

751
00:41:21,280 --> 00:41:28,280
Suppose I need money, I need a hundred dollars,

752
00:41:28,280 --> 00:41:30,280
desperately.

753
00:41:30,280 --> 00:41:34,280
And I know I can't pay it back anytime soon.

754
00:41:34,280 --> 00:41:36,280
I come to you and make

755
00:41:36,280 --> 00:41:38,280
you a promise, a false promise,

756
00:41:38,280 --> 00:41:40,280
one I know I can't keep.

757
00:41:40,280 --> 00:41:42,280
Please give me a hundred dollars today,

758
00:41:42,280 --> 00:41:46,280
lend me the money, I will repay you next week.

759
00:41:46,280 --> 00:41:49,280
Is that consistent

760
00:41:49,280 --> 00:41:51,280
with the categorical imperative, that false promise?

761
00:41:51,280 --> 00:41:54,280
Kant says, no.

762
00:41:54,280 --> 00:41:58,280
And the test, the way we can determine

763
00:41:58,280 --> 00:42:00,780
that the false promise is at

764
00:42:00,780 --> 00:42:03,280
odds with the categorical imperative is,

765
00:42:03,280 --> 00:42:06,280
try to universalize it.

766
00:42:06,280 --> 00:42:09,280
Universalize the maxim upon which you're about to act.

767
00:42:10,280 --> 00:42:14,280
If everybody made false promises when they needed money,

768
00:42:14,280 --> 00:42:19,280
then nobody would believe those promises,

769
00:42:19,280 --> 00:42:23,280
there would be no such thing as a promise,

770
00:42:23,280 --> 00:42:26,280
and so there would be a contradiction.

771
00:42:26,280 --> 00:42:32,280
The maxim universalized would undermine itself.

772
00:42:32,280 --> 00:42:35,280
That's the test.

773
00:42:35,280 --> 00:42:37,280
That's how we can know

774
00:42:37,280 --> 00:42:39,280
that the false promise is wrong.

775
00:42:40,280 --> 00:42:46,280
Well, what about the formula of the universal law?

776
00:42:46,280 --> 00:42:49,280
You find it persuasive?

777
00:42:54,280 --> 00:42:56,280
What do you think?

778
00:42:56,280 --> 00:42:57,280
Go ahead.

779
00:42:57,280 --> 00:43:00,280
I have a question about the difference between

780
00:43:00,280 --> 00:43:03,280
categoricalism and a hypothesis that

781
00:43:03,280 --> 00:43:06,280
if you're going to act...

782
00:43:06,280 --> 00:43:08,280
Between categorical and hypothetical...

783
00:43:08,280 --> 00:43:09,280
Hypothetical, yeah.

784
00:43:09,280 --> 00:43:10,280
Imperatives.

785
00:43:10,280 --> 00:43:11,280
Right.

786
00:43:11,280 --> 00:43:15,280
If you're going to act with a categorical imperative

787
00:43:15,280 --> 00:43:18,280
so that the maxim doesn't undermine itself,

788
00:43:18,280 --> 00:43:20,780
it sounds like I am going

789
00:43:20,780 --> 00:43:23,280
to do X because I want Y.

790
00:43:23,280 --> 00:43:27,280
I am going to not lie in dire need

791
00:43:27,280 --> 00:43:29,780
because I want the world to function

792
00:43:29,780 --> 00:43:32,280
in such a way that promises are kept.

793
00:43:32,280 --> 00:43:35,280
I don't want to liquidate the practice of promises.

794
00:43:35,280 --> 00:43:36,280
Right.

795
00:43:36,280 --> 00:43:37,280
It sounds like justifying...

796
00:43:37,280 --> 00:43:38,280
Right.

797
00:43:38,280 --> 00:43:41,280
Justifying a means by an ends.

798
00:43:41,280 --> 00:43:43,280
It seems like an instance

799
00:43:43,280 --> 00:43:45,280
of consequentialist reasoning, you're saying.

800
00:43:45,280 --> 00:43:46,280
Right.

801
00:43:46,280 --> 00:43:47,280
And what's your name?

802
00:43:47,280 --> 00:43:48,280
Tim.

803
00:43:48,280 --> 00:43:49,280
Tim.

804
00:43:53,280 --> 00:43:56,280
Well, Tim, John Stuart Mill agreed with you.

805
00:43:56,280 --> 00:44:01,280
He made this criticism of Kant.

806
00:44:01,280 --> 00:44:06,280
He said, if I universalize the maxim and find

807
00:44:06,280 --> 00:44:08,780
that the whole practice of promise

808
00:44:08,780 --> 00:44:11,280
keeping would be destroyed if universalized,

809
00:44:11,280 --> 00:44:15,280
I must be appealing somehow to consequences.

810
00:44:15,280 --> 00:44:16,280
Right.

811
00:44:16,280 --> 00:44:22,280
If that's the reason not to tell a false promise.

812
00:44:22,280 --> 00:44:27,280
So John Stuart Mill agreed with that criticism against Kant.

813
00:44:27,280 --> 00:44:29,280
But John Stuart Mill was wrong.

814
00:44:31,280 --> 00:44:34,280
You're in good company, though.

815
00:44:34,280 --> 00:44:36,280
You're in good company, Tim.

816
00:44:36,280 --> 00:44:43,280
Kant is often read, as Tim just read him,

817
00:44:43,280 --> 00:44:45,280
as appealing to consequences.

818
00:44:45,280 --> 00:44:50,280
The world would be worse off if everybody lied

819
00:44:50,280 --> 00:44:53,280
because then no one could rely on anybody else's word.

820
00:44:53,280 --> 00:44:56,280
Therefore, you shouldn't lie.

821
00:44:56,280 --> 00:44:59,280
That's not what Kant is saying exactly.

822
00:44:59,280 --> 00:45:03,280
Although it's easy to interpret him as saying that.

823
00:45:04,280 --> 00:45:06,780
I think what he's saying

824
00:45:06,780 --> 00:45:09,280
is that this is the test.

825
00:45:09,280 --> 00:45:14,280
This is the test of whether the maxim corresponds

826
00:45:14,280 --> 00:45:16,280
with the categorical imperative.

827
00:45:16,280 --> 00:45:19,280
It isn't exactly the reason.

828
00:45:19,280 --> 00:45:21,280
It's not the reason.

829
00:45:21,280 --> 00:45:26,280
The reason you should universalize to test your maxim

830
00:45:26,280 --> 00:45:33,280
is to see whether you are privileging your particular needs

831
00:45:33,280 --> 00:45:38,280
and desires over everybody else's.

832
00:45:38,280 --> 00:45:41,280
It's a way of pointing to this feature,

833
00:45:41,280 --> 00:45:44,280
this demand of the categorical imperative,

834
00:45:44,280 --> 00:45:48,280
that the reasons for your actions shouldn't depend

835
00:45:48,280 --> 00:45:52,280
for their justification on your interests,

836
00:45:52,280 --> 00:45:55,280
your needs, your special circumstances

837
00:45:55,280 --> 00:45:59,280
being more important than somebody else's.

838
00:45:59,280 --> 00:46:01,280
That, I think, is the moral intuition

839
00:46:01,280 --> 00:46:03,280
lying behind the universalization.

840
00:46:03,280 --> 00:46:05,280
That's the moral test.

841
00:46:05,280 --> 00:46:07,280
So let me spell out the second,

842
00:46:07,280 --> 00:46:11,280
Kant's second version of the categorical imperative,

843
00:46:11,280 --> 00:46:16,280
perhaps in a way that's more intuitively accessible

844
00:46:16,280 --> 00:46:20,280
than the formula of universal law.

845
00:46:20,280 --> 00:46:26,280
It's the formula of humanity as an end.

846
00:46:26,280 --> 00:46:29,280
Kant introduces the second version

847
00:46:29,280 --> 00:46:31,280
of the categorical imperative

848
00:46:31,280 --> 00:46:33,280
with the following line of argument.

849
00:46:33,280 --> 00:46:36,280
We can't base the categorical imperative

850
00:46:36,280 --> 00:46:40,280
on any particular interest, purposes, or ends,

851
00:46:40,280 --> 00:46:43,280
because then it would be only relative

852
00:46:43,280 --> 00:46:46,280
to the person whose ends they were.

853
00:46:46,280 --> 00:46:49,280
But suppose there were something

854
00:46:49,280 --> 00:46:54,280
whose existence has in itself an absolute value,

855
00:46:54,280 --> 00:46:56,280
an end in itself.

856
00:46:56,280 --> 00:47:00,280
Then in it, and in it alone,

857
00:47:00,280 --> 00:47:02,280
would there be the ground

858
00:47:02,280 --> 00:47:05,280
of a possible categorical imperative?

859
00:47:05,280 --> 00:47:09,280
Well, what is there that we can think of

860
00:47:09,280 --> 00:47:14,280
as having its end in itself?

861
00:47:14,280 --> 00:47:16,280
Kant's answer is this.

862
00:47:16,280 --> 00:47:19,280
I say that man, and in general,

863
00:47:19,280 --> 00:47:21,280
every rational being,

864
00:47:21,280 --> 00:47:24,280
exists as an end in himself,

865
00:47:24,280 --> 00:47:27,280
not merely as a means for arbitrary use

866
00:47:27,280 --> 00:47:30,280
by this or that will.

867
00:47:30,280 --> 00:47:32,280
And here Kant distinguishes

868
00:47:32,280 --> 00:47:34,280
between persons on the one hand

869
00:47:34,280 --> 00:47:37,280
and things on the other.

870
00:47:37,280 --> 00:47:40,280
Rational beings are persons.

871
00:47:40,280 --> 00:47:44,280
They don't just have a relative value for us,

872
00:47:44,280 --> 00:47:46,280
but if anything has,

873
00:47:46,280 --> 00:47:48,280
they have an absolute value,

874
00:47:48,280 --> 00:47:50,280
an intrinsic value.

875
00:47:50,280 --> 00:47:53,280
That is, rational beings have dignity.

876
00:47:53,280 --> 00:47:56,280
They're worthy of reverence and respect.

877
00:47:56,280 --> 00:47:59,280
This line of reasoning leads Kant

878
00:47:59,280 --> 00:48:01,280
to the second formulation

879
00:48:01,280 --> 00:48:02,280
of the categorical imperative,

880
00:48:02,280 --> 00:48:04,280
which is this.

881
00:48:04,280 --> 00:48:06,280
Act in such a way

882
00:48:06,280 --> 00:48:08,280
that you always treat humanity,

883
00:48:08,280 --> 00:48:10,280
whether in your own person

884
00:48:10,280 --> 00:48:13,280
or in the person of any other,

885
00:48:13,280 --> 00:48:16,280
never simply as a means,

886
00:48:16,280 --> 00:48:21,280
but always at the same time as an end.

887
00:48:21,280 --> 00:48:23,280
So that's the formula

888
00:48:23,280 --> 00:48:27,280
of humanity as an end.

889
00:48:27,280 --> 00:48:28,280
The idea that human beings,

890
00:48:28,280 --> 00:48:30,280
as rational beings,

891
00:48:30,280 --> 00:48:32,280
are ends in themselves,

892
00:48:32,280 --> 00:48:35,280
not open to use,

893
00:48:35,280 --> 00:48:38,280
merely as a means.

894
00:48:38,280 --> 00:48:41,280
When I make a false promise to you,

895
00:48:41,280 --> 00:48:46,280
I'm using you as a means

896
00:48:46,280 --> 00:48:48,280
to my ends,

897
00:48:48,280 --> 00:48:50,280
to my desire for the hundred dollars.

898
00:48:50,280 --> 00:48:55,280
And so I'm failing to respect you.

899
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:58,280
I'm failing to respect your dignity.

900
00:48:58,280 --> 00:49:01,280
I'm manipulating you.

901
00:49:01,280 --> 00:49:03,280
Now consider the example

902
00:49:03,280 --> 00:49:08,280
of the duty against suicide.

903
00:49:08,280 --> 00:49:12,280
Murder and suicide

904
00:49:12,280 --> 00:49:15,280
are at odds with the categorical imperative.

905
00:49:15,280 --> 00:49:17,280
Why?

906
00:49:17,280 --> 00:49:19,280
If I murder someone,

907
00:49:19,280 --> 00:49:23,280
I'm taking their life for some purpose,

908
00:49:23,280 --> 00:49:27,280
either because I'm a hired killer,

909
00:49:27,280 --> 00:49:29,280
or I'm in the throes

910
00:49:29,280 --> 00:49:31,280
of some great anger or passion.

911
00:49:31,280 --> 00:49:32,280
Well, I have some interest,

912
00:49:32,280 --> 00:49:35,280
some purpose that's particular

913
00:49:35,280 --> 00:49:38,280
for the sake of which I'm using them

914
00:49:38,280 --> 00:49:39,280
as a means.

915
00:49:39,280 --> 00:49:41,280
Murder violates

916
00:49:41,280 --> 00:49:45,280
the categorical imperative.

917
00:49:45,280 --> 00:49:47,280
For Kant, morally speaking,

918
00:49:47,280 --> 00:49:50,280
suicide is on a par with murder.

919
00:49:50,280 --> 00:49:51,280
It's on a par with murder

920
00:49:51,280 --> 00:49:55,280
because what we violate

921
00:49:55,280 --> 00:49:57,280
when we take a life,

922
00:49:57,280 --> 00:49:59,280
when we take someone's life,

923
00:49:59,280 --> 00:50:01,280
ours or somebody else's,

924
00:50:01,280 --> 00:50:04,280
we use that person,

925
00:50:04,280 --> 00:50:06,280
we use a rational being,

926
00:50:06,280 --> 00:50:09,280
we use humanity as a means,

927
00:50:09,280 --> 00:50:12,280
and so we fail to respect humanity

928
00:50:12,280 --> 00:50:14,280
as an end.

929
00:50:14,280 --> 00:50:16,280
And that capacity for reason,

930
00:50:16,280 --> 00:50:18,280
that humanity

931
00:50:18,280 --> 00:50:21,280
that commands respect,

932
00:50:21,280 --> 00:50:23,280
that is the ground of dignity,

933
00:50:23,280 --> 00:50:25,280
that humanity,

934
00:50:25,280 --> 00:50:27,280
that capacity for reason,

935
00:50:27,280 --> 00:50:30,280
resides undifferentiated

936
00:50:30,280 --> 00:50:31,280
in all of us.

937
00:50:31,280 --> 00:50:34,280
And so I violate that dignity

938
00:50:34,280 --> 00:50:36,280
in my own person

939
00:50:36,280 --> 00:50:38,280
if I commit suicide,

940
00:50:38,280 --> 00:50:39,280
and in murder

941
00:50:39,280 --> 00:50:41,280
if I take somebody else's life.

942
00:50:41,280 --> 00:50:43,280
From a moral point of view,

943
00:50:43,280 --> 00:50:45,280
they're the same.

944
00:50:45,280 --> 00:50:47,280
And the reason they're the same

945
00:50:47,280 --> 00:50:51,280
has to do with the universal character

946
00:50:51,280 --> 00:50:55,280
and ground of the moral law.

947
00:50:55,280 --> 00:50:58,280
The reason that we have to respect

948
00:50:58,280 --> 00:51:00,280
the dignity of other people

949
00:51:00,280 --> 00:51:02,280
has not to do with anything

950
00:51:02,280 --> 00:51:05,280
in particular about them.

951
00:51:05,280 --> 00:51:07,280
And so respect, Kantian respect,

952
00:51:07,280 --> 00:51:11,280
is unlike love in this way.

953
00:51:11,280 --> 00:51:14,280
It's unlike sympathy.

954
00:51:14,280 --> 00:51:16,280
It's unlike solidarity

955
00:51:16,280 --> 00:51:19,280
or fellow feeling or altruism.

956
00:51:19,280 --> 00:51:21,280
Because love and those other

957
00:51:21,280 --> 00:51:23,280
particular virtues or reasons

958
00:51:23,280 --> 00:51:24,280
for caring about other people

959
00:51:24,280 --> 00:51:26,280
have to do with who they are

960
00:51:26,280 --> 00:51:28,280
in particular.

961
00:51:28,280 --> 00:51:30,280
But respect for Kant,

962
00:51:30,280 --> 00:51:34,280
respect is respect for humanity

963
00:51:34,280 --> 00:51:36,280
which is universal,

964
00:51:36,280 --> 00:51:37,280
for a rational capacity

965
00:51:37,280 --> 00:51:39,280
which is universal.

966
00:51:39,280 --> 00:51:41,280
And that's why violating it

967
00:51:41,280 --> 00:51:42,280
in my own case

968
00:51:42,280 --> 00:51:44,280
is as objectionable

969
00:51:44,280 --> 00:51:45,280
as violating it

970
00:51:45,280 --> 00:51:47,280
in the case of any other.

971
00:51:47,280 --> 00:51:49,280
Questions or objections?

972
00:51:54,280 --> 00:51:57,280
Go ahead.

973
00:51:57,280 --> 00:51:59,280
I guess I'm somewhat worried

974
00:51:59,280 --> 00:52:04,280
about Kant's statement

975
00:52:04,280 --> 00:52:06,280
that you cannot use a person

976
00:52:06,280 --> 00:52:07,280
as a means because every person

977
00:52:07,280 --> 00:52:10,280
is an end in and of themselves.

978
00:52:10,280 --> 00:52:12,280
Because it seems that

979
00:52:12,280 --> 00:52:13,280
every day in order to get

980
00:52:13,280 --> 00:52:14,280
something accomplished

981
00:52:14,280 --> 00:52:16,280
for that day,

982
00:52:16,280 --> 00:52:18,280
I must use myself as a means

983
00:52:18,280 --> 00:52:20,280
to some end.

984
00:52:20,280 --> 00:52:21,280
And I must use the people around me

985
00:52:21,280 --> 00:52:23,280
as a means to some end as well.

986
00:52:24,280 --> 00:52:28,280
For instance, suppose that

987
00:52:28,280 --> 00:52:29,280
I want to do well in a class

988
00:52:29,280 --> 00:52:31,280
and I have to write a paper.

989
00:52:31,280 --> 00:52:32,280
I have to use myself as a means

990
00:52:32,280 --> 00:52:34,280
to write the paper.

991
00:52:34,280 --> 00:52:37,280
Suppose I want to buy something.

992
00:52:37,280 --> 00:52:38,280
Food.

993
00:52:38,280 --> 00:52:39,280
I must go to the store

994
00:52:39,280 --> 00:52:40,280
and use the person

995
00:52:40,280 --> 00:52:41,280
working behind the counter

996
00:52:41,280 --> 00:52:42,280
as a means for me

997
00:52:42,280 --> 00:52:43,280
to purchase my food.

998
00:52:43,280 --> 00:52:45,280
Right.

999
00:52:45,280 --> 00:52:46,280
That's true.

1000
00:52:46,280 --> 00:52:47,280
You do.

1001
00:52:47,280 --> 00:52:48,280
What's your name?

1002
00:52:48,280 --> 00:52:49,280
Patrick.

1003
00:52:49,280 --> 00:52:50,280
Patrick, you're not doing

1004
00:52:50,280 --> 00:52:52,280
anything wrong.

1005
00:52:52,280 --> 00:52:53,280
You're not violating

1006
00:52:53,280 --> 00:52:55,280
the categorical imperative

1007
00:52:55,280 --> 00:52:56,280
when you use other people

1008
00:52:56,280 --> 00:52:57,280
as means.

1009
00:52:57,280 --> 00:52:58,280
That's not objectionable

1010
00:52:58,280 --> 00:53:02,280
provided

1011
00:53:02,280 --> 00:53:04,280
when we deal with other people

1012
00:53:04,280 --> 00:53:05,280
for the sake of advancing

1013
00:53:05,280 --> 00:53:07,280
our projects and purposes

1014
00:53:07,280 --> 00:53:08,280
and interests,

1015
00:53:08,280 --> 00:53:10,280
which we all do,

1016
00:53:10,280 --> 00:53:13,280
provided we treat them

1017
00:53:13,280 --> 00:53:16,280
in a way that is consistent

1018
00:53:16,280 --> 00:53:20,280
with respect for their dignity.

1019
00:53:20,280 --> 00:53:22,280
And what it means to respect them

1020
00:53:22,280 --> 00:53:31,280
is given by the categorical imperative.

1021
00:53:31,280 --> 00:53:33,280
Are you persuaded?

1022
00:53:33,280 --> 00:53:38,280
Do you think that Kant has given

1023
00:53:38,280 --> 00:53:39,280
a compelling account,

1024
00:53:39,280 --> 00:53:41,280
a persuasive account,

1025
00:53:41,280 --> 00:53:47,280
of the supreme principle of morality?

1026
00:53:47,280 --> 00:53:50,280
Reread the groundwork

1027
00:53:50,280 --> 00:53:51,280
and we'll try to answer

1028
00:53:51,280 --> 00:53:52,280
that question.

1029
00:53:52,280 --> 00:53:53,280
We'll answer that question

1030
00:53:53,280 --> 00:53:54,280
next time.

1031
00:53:55,280 --> 00:53:56,280
Thank you.

